{
    // Zero-Coupon Bond Offer - a lender's standing offer to lend, closed by any borrower
    // BondNFT_Generator mints the bond asset into this box: tokens(0) is the bond's future singleton, and
    // everything else in the box is the principal. A borrower closes the offer by creating the exact bond
    // the lender asked for, carrying the singleton and the collateral, and keeps the principal.
    // One contract serves ERG and token loans: CONST_BOND_HASH says which bond, the box holds the principal.
    // The bond always pays the BondAssetVault, so the bond asset always unlocks the repayment or collateral.
    //
    // CONSTANTS
    // Every offer is this one ErgoTree template with different constants, so UIs find offers by template
    // hash and read the terms from the constants. Clients stamp offers out of the template by replacing
    // constants rather than recompiling: replacement keeps the template byte for byte by construction,
    // while a recompile keeps it only as long as the compiler lays the constants out the same way.
    // A template hash leaves every constant out, not only the terms, so verifying an offer means also
    // checking that each constant that is not a term (CONST_VAULT_HASH among them) is the template's own.
    //
    // Fixed, the same in every offer:
    // CONST_VAULT_HASH:        Coll[Byte] - blake2b256 of BondAssetVault. Bond R8.
    //
    // The lender's terms:
    // CONST_BOND_HASH:         Coll[Byte] - blake2b256 of the bond ErgoTree: ZC_ERG_Bond, or ZC_Token_Bond
    //                                       compiled for the repayment token
    // CONST_REPAYMENT:         Long       - bond R6(0)
    // CONST_BATCHING_FEE:      Long       - bond R6(1)
    // CONST_MATURITY:          Int        - bond R7, an absolute height
    // CONST_LIQ_SCRIPT_HASH:   Coll[Byte] - bond R9
    // CONST_COLLATERAL_ERG:    Long       - the least nanoERG the bond may hold
    // CONST_COLLATERAL_TOKENS: Coll[(Coll[Byte], Long)] - the bond's collateral tokens, exactly
    //
    // TOKENS
    // tokens(0):  the bond asset's second unit, from the generator
    // tokens(1..): principal tokens, for a token loan
    //
    // PATHS
    // Close:  OUTPUTS(0) is the bond. Nothing else is constrained: whoever posts the collateral is the
    //         borrower, so the principal goes wherever they send it.
    // Cancel: the holder of the circulating unit spends it alongside, and both units are burned. That unit
    //         is the lender's claim, so presenting it is the authorization, even after it was sold.
    //
    // ASSUMPTIONS
    // - No registers are read. The generator committed to this box's whole ErgoTree, terms included, and
    //   refused registers on it, so the executor who minted could not choose any term.
    // - tokens(0) is a verified bond asset only because the generator put it there. An offer box built by
    //   hand proves nothing: verify an offer by the generator its bond asset came from.
    // - One offer closes per transaction, since each needs OUTPUTS(0) to carry its own singleton.
    // - Maturity is not compared to HEIGHT. Closing at or past maturity makes a bond that can be liquidated
    //   at once, and only the borrower who closed it loses by that.
    // - The bond and the principal are not checked against each other. A token offer whose bond repays in
    //   another token is the lender's own mistake; the client must build them as a pair.

    // Helper Methods
    def numTokenInOutputs(tokenId: Coll[Byte]): Long = {
        OUTPUTS.fold(0L, {
            (a: Long, b: Box) =>
                val totalTokens = b.tokens.fold(0L, {
                    (z: Long, t: (Coll[Byte], Long)) => if(t._1 == tokenId) z + t._2 else z
                })
                a + totalTokens
        })
    }
    def numTokenInInputs(tokenId: Coll[Byte]): Long = {
        INPUTS.fold(0L, {
            (a: Long, b: Box) =>
                val totalTokens = b.tokens.fold(0L, {
                    (z: Long, t: (Coll[Byte], Long)) => if(t._1 == tokenId) z + t._2 else z
                })
                a + totalTokens
        })
    }

    val bondAssetId = SELF.tokens(0)._1

    if (numTokenInOutputs(bondAssetId) > 0L) {
        // Close
        val bondBox     = OUTPUTS(0)
        val bondAmounts = bondBox.R6[Coll[Long]].get

        val validBond =
            blake2b256(bondBox.propositionBytes) == CONST_BOND_HASH &&
            bondBox.tokens.size > 0 &&
            bondBox.tokens(0)._1 == bondAssetId &&
            bondBox.tokens(0)._2 == 1L &&
            bondBox.tokens.slice(1, bondBox.tokens.size) == CONST_COLLATERAL_TOKENS &&
            bondBox.value >= CONST_COLLATERAL_ERG

        val validTerms =
            bondBox.R4[Coll[Byte]].get == SELF.id &&
            // The borrower's own choice, but it must read as Coll[Byte], or the bond could never be spent
            // and the lender could not liquidate it
            bondBox.R5[Coll[Byte]].isDefined &&
            bondAmounts.size == 2 &&
            bondAmounts(0) == CONST_REPAYMENT &&
            bondAmounts(1) == CONST_BATCHING_FEE &&
            bondBox.R7[Int].get == CONST_MATURITY &&
            bondBox.R8[Coll[Byte]].get == CONST_VAULT_HASH &&
            bondBox.R9[Coll[Byte]].get == CONST_LIQ_SCRIPT_HASH

        // The bond trusts its creator to have bounded the fee. Read off the bond, not the constant, so the
        // compiler cannot fold the check away and change the template.
        val feeBounded =
            bondAmounts(1) >= 0L &&
            bondBox.value - bondAmounts(1) >= 1000000L

        sigmaProp(validBond && validTerms && feeBounded)
    } else {
        // Cancel. Both units are already burned here, since none reached an output.
        sigmaProp(numTokenInInputs(bondAssetId) >= 2L)
    }
}
